WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.861
(slow piano music)

00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:05.069
- You gotta be a certain type of person

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:07.589
to get into flying without an engine.

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:10.000
(bouncy, upbeat music)

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.069
The fact that we're able
to leave the ground,

00:00:12.069 --> 00:00:14.786
leave everything behind,
and fly through the skies

00:00:14.786 --> 00:00:17.869
on nylon and strings, is exceptional.

00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:28.210
(bouncy piano music)

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.115
The Rat Race is a seven day
long paragliding competition

00:00:31.115 --> 00:00:33.436
with 200 participants, that also serves

00:00:33.436 --> 00:00:35.787
as the U.S. National
Paragliding Championship.

00:00:35.787 --> 00:00:38.939
(wind rumbling)

00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:39.928
My name is Nick Greece.

00:00:39.928 --> 00:00:42.881
I'm a professional paraglider pilot.

00:00:42.881 --> 00:00:45.901
A paragliding race is very
similar to a sailboat race.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:47.331
They race around buoys.

00:00:47.331 --> 00:00:49.601
We race around several
different GPS coordinates

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:51.572
that we have to hit along the way.

00:00:51.572 --> 00:00:53.063
Each morning, the race course is determined

00:00:53.063 --> 00:00:55.522
by the weather for that day,
and a course typically ranges

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.605
from 40 to 70 miles long.

00:00:59.302 --> 00:01:00.849
The first person to finish
the course every day

00:01:00.849 --> 00:01:02.202
gets 1,000 points.

00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:05.314
If someone's five seconds
back, they get 995

00:01:05.314 --> 00:01:07.325
and so on and so forth.

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:10.573
And then at seven days, you
add up all your scores together

00:01:10.573 --> 00:01:13.656
and whoever has the most points wins.

00:01:15.358 --> 00:01:19.261
I grew up in Long Island in a
suburb close to New York City.

00:01:19.261 --> 00:01:21.035
I went to Beloit College in Wisconsin,

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:22.673
and after that I returned to Manhattan

00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:25.409
to take a job at a PR firm.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:28.620
My second day at work
was September 11th, 2001,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.637
so I was walking to work
and I saw the first plane

00:01:31.637 --> 00:01:34.428
hit the World Trade
Center from the street.

00:01:34.428 --> 00:01:37.222
I don't know if that
played a significant role,

00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:39.694
but it played some role in me deciding

00:01:39.694 --> 00:01:43.010
that I needed to do
something sooner than later

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.959
if I wasn't completely
satisfied with what I was doing.

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:48.773
So I sold all my possessions
and lived out of my car,

00:01:48.773 --> 00:01:50.818
and all I did was fly.

00:01:50.818 --> 00:01:54.066
I've dedicated my life since
2001 to flying paragliders.

00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:56.523
For many years I've been one
of the top pilots in the U.S.,

00:01:56.523 --> 00:01:59.523
and that to me is a sign of success.

00:02:01.766 --> 00:02:03.288
Watching the start of a paragliding race

00:02:03.288 --> 00:02:04.897
is a sight to behold.

00:02:04.897 --> 00:02:07.891
100 pilots launching off
a hill and then getting up

00:02:07.891 --> 00:02:11.624
into the starting paddock,
which is what we call a gaggle.

00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:13.483
The start, it can be a chaotic experience

00:02:13.483 --> 00:02:16.104
of people that are
trying to get to the top

00:02:16.104 --> 00:02:18.213
so that they're in the best position,

00:02:18.213 --> 00:02:21.213
so everybody jockeying for position.

00:02:22.515 --> 00:02:25.227
Once that starts, it's
just a glorious experience

00:02:25.227 --> 00:02:28.856
of rallying through mountain
ranges for a whole day

00:02:28.856 --> 00:02:30.356
with your friends.

00:02:31.838 --> 00:02:34.067
After seven days, you're pretty exhausted,

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.809
but that's also the real joy in doing

00:02:36.809 --> 00:02:39.624
any kind of endurance event.

00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:41.918
Just like with anything,
whether you're trading stocks

00:02:41.918 --> 00:02:45.703
or riding bikes, or flying paragliders,

00:02:45.703 --> 00:02:47.973
you're always trying to make progress

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:51.056
and increase your ability to succeed.

00:02:52.175 --> 00:02:55.544
And paragliding competition
is a beautiful example

00:02:55.544 --> 00:02:58.955
of pitting your skills, not
only against your competitors,

00:02:58.955 --> 00:03:00.387
but against the elements.

00:03:00.387 --> 00:03:03.637
(lively, bouncy music)

00:03:08.092 --> 00:03:10.259
(chiming)

